in today's morning rounds a scientific  breakthrough that may eventually allow  many blind people to see a handful of  volunteers are already seeing the  promise of a new device about 18 months  into a five-year clinical study Jonathan  Vigliotti shows us the technology and  how it could transform their lives I  always had a little out hope for Jason  ester Huson was that one day he might  see again the South African lost his  sight in a car accident when he was just  23 years old lost control of the car the  car hit the curb and I just hit the  steering wheel and the the window and  went out the sunroof injuries from the  crash left Jason in a world of total  darkness I think in the first couple of  years of being blind it was just you  have to accept the fact that you're  blind until a friend of his heard about  a clinical trial only for people who  could once see but became blind starting  half a world away at the University of  California Los Angeles  it's a brand new device it's never been  used in humans before dr. nader parot  ian leads the study of an experimental  device called the Orion here's how it  works using a camera attached to a pair  of sunglasses  Orion captures images a person would see  and sends it through a handheld device  to an implant that's been surgically  inserted into the visual part of the  brain the implant then turns those  images into dots of light that the  patient can see they can be various  shapes it could be a circle it could be  an oval it could be a moving line Jason  is one of only six people in the world  who qualified for the study and to  receive the implant the first time that  I saw a little white dot I was  speechless it was the most beautiful  thing I've ever seen you can actually  see the electrode array while not  restoring sight in the traditional sense  what he can see are flashes of light if  I look around I can perceive movement I  can see some light and dark I can tell  you whether a line is vertical hole  Anto or at a 45-degree angle yeah the  device not only allowed Jason to regain  a small part of his vision but some true  independence I cook I clean I can take  out the trash I can sew it laundry but  just the darks are from the lights no  color yet even going for a stroll  outside by himself if I looked down I  can see flashing lights in fact this  study has gone so well in Jason's case  that sometimes even he doesn't realize  how amazing it's been he nonchalantly  was telling me about how he was at the  bar there's a very dark dive bar the  bartender was wearing a white shirt you  can see the bartender walking towards  him and and I just saw two little dots  lighting up and then three and then five  and then she was right in front of me  and she was all shiny and I thought wow  that's cool right you know I said you  know do you realize what you just said a  glimpse into the future that could give  countless people a second chance at  sight for CBS this morning Jonathan  Vigliotti Los Angeles amazing stop it  gives you hope stuff we take for granted  that's really amazing
 

